3. At that time he was lying on a couch in the Thessalos in the patriarchate, a triclinium so named, where he received the imperial message. As soon as he heard the words he was struck to the soul. He sent the bearer away at once, saying only that he would consider the matter carefully. Quicker than a word, he took a knife and cut the veins of his belly. He knew the copious flow of blood would cause fear and pity in most people, but no death or danger. At once a rumour and an outcry spread around the church, and even reached the imperial ears ahead of the droungarios: the empress had ordered the patriarch slaughtered. The patrician Bardas was sent to examine the reports closely. He discovered that the wounds had somehow been made deliberately, and the play-acting was exposed. His own servants informed against him, and the cutting instruments, which were lancets, were produced for all to see. From then on [?], caught on such a charge and with such self-murderous intent, the scoundrel was driven out and expelled from the Church and confined to his suburban estate called Psicha. In his place was brought in a man who, through many labours and toils, had kept his virtue intact. A long imprisonment, in a rotting dungeon it seems, had left him with the loss of his hair, and from God and from the empress Theodora he received the government and steering of the Church. This was Methodius, the great and invincible champion of the Church.

4. Consider what the noble lady did, who had truly been a helper to her husband and not an opponent. She gathered together the orthodox from wherever on earth they were and decreed that they could speak freely again. Then she said: "Clergy of God and Fathers, I am granting you the restoration of the icons with all goodwill. In return, be willing to grant me, your sovereign, a just favour. It is not a lowly or small one, which would hardly count as a favour, nor one unfitting or unprofitable either for you who give it or for me who ask it. It is a favour that is proper, lasting and acceptable to God. It is this: that pardon, forgiveness and amnesty from God extend to my husband and emperor in these matters, for such a sin;

