25. Since the emperor could find no further means or pretext for doing away with Basil, he turned to another plan, wicked and lawless. He decided to bring yet another partner into the imperial office. There was a man called Basilikinos, himself one of that murderous council, a base and foul man, effeminate and fond of revels, from Nicomedia, full brother of Constantine Kapnogenes, who was later entrusted with the office of eparch; at that time he was enrolled among the rowers of the imperial trireme. On one occasion Michael dressed this ill-named Basilikinos in the celebrated imperial purple, the conspicuous and enviable crown, the all-gold chlamys, the scarlet jeweled shoes, and the other insignia of imperial rank, and led him out to the senate, holding him by the hand and attending on him, as Nero once did with the notorious Eros. And he said, word for word: "Look, all of you, and marvel. Is it not fitting that he should be emperor?

First, a form worthy of sovereign rule;
Second, the crown sits naturally on him;
And everything befits the dignity."

And also: "How much better it would have been to make him emperor instead of Basil!" All those in the palace who saw and heard this were struck dumb, astounded at the emperor's senseless stupefaction and derangement born of folly. So utterly had the man, through constant drunkenness and lawless and lewd deeds, departed from all that was proper, so crazed and frenzied had he become.
